'''upholder''' (up-hōl′dėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''upholdere'', a dealer; < ''up'' + ''holder''. Cf. ''upholdster''.] '''1†.''' One who undertakes or carries on a business; a tradesman; a broker; a dealer, especially a dealer in small wares.
{{smaller block|''Vpholderes'' on the hul [Cornhill] shullen haue hit to selle.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xiii. 218.}}
''Vpholdere'', that sellythe smal thyngys. Velaber, velabra.
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 512.}}}}
'''2†.''' An undertaker; one who has charge of funerals.
{{smaller block|<poem>Th’ ''upholder'', rueful harbinger of death,
Waits with impatience for the dying breath.</poem>
{{right|''Gay'', Trivia.}}}}
'''3†.''' An upholsterer.
{{smaller block|Birchover, otherwise Birchin, Lane, in the reign of Henry VI., “had ye for the most part dwelling Fripperers or ''Upholders'', that sold old apparel and household stuff” (Stow, “Annals,” p. 75, ed. 1876).
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., X. 328.}}}}
'''4.''' One who upholds; a supporter; a defender; a sustainer: as, an ''upholder'' of religious freedom.
{{smaller block|An earnest and zealous ''upholder'' of his country.
{{right|''Holinshed'', Chron. of Ireland, an. 1546.}}}}

'''upholdster†''', '''upholster†''', ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''upholstar''; < late ME. ''upholdster'', ''upholster''; < ''uphold'' + ''-ster''.] An upholder or upholsterer.
{{smaller block|''Upholdsters''—vieswariers.—Euerard the ''vpholster'' can well stoppe a mantel hooled, full agayn, carde agayn, skowre agayn a goune, and alle old cloth.
{{right|''Caxton'', Booke for Travellers (quoted in Prompt. Parv.,}}
{{right|[p. 512, note).}}}}

